<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n30454" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">nătātōrĭus</span></b> , a,
			um, adj.
		natator, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>of</i> or <i>belonging to a swimmer,
				that serves to swim with, natatory</i> (post-class.): 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">cortex,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Isid. Orig. 17, 7, 27</span></i>
			.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<i>Subst.</i>: <b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">nătātōrĭa</span></b> ,
				ae, f., and <b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">nătātōrĭum</span></b> ,
				i, n., <i>a place for
				swimming, a swimming - place, a bath; a pool</i>,
					<i><span class="en">Sid. Ep. 2, 2</span></i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">vade ad natatoria Siloe,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Vulg. Joh. 9, 7</span></i>
			; <i><span class="en">11</span></i>.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
